{"id": "1.2-00024", "case_id": "github_secworks_aes_direct_v1", "parent_id": null, "source": "github/secworks/aes", "record_protocol": null, "panels": {"stored": "<h2>Agent task and scoring</h2><div class='card'><table><tbody><tr><th style='width:175px'>Input task</th><td class='path'>Project specification, interfaces, and permitted public assets</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Agent context</th><td class='path'>Direct agent workspace: <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00024&amp;file_q=public%2F'>public/</a> (5 files)</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Expected output</th><td class='path'>Required RTL: src/rtl/aes.v, src/rtl/aes_core.v, src/rtl/aes_decipher_block.v, src/rtl/aes_encipher_block.v, src/rtl/aes_inv_sbox.v, src/rtl/aes_key_mem.v, src/rtl/aes_sbox.v. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00024&amp;file=public%2Foutput_contract.json'>Open full output contract</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Hidden reference</th><td class='path'>7 RTL files under private/reference/; <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00024&amp;file=private%2Freference%2Fsrc%2Frtl%2Faes.v'>open first reference file</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Private score</th><td class='path'>Passed verification testpoints divided by total testpoints, using testbenches or formal verification<br><strong>5 verification testpoints:</strong> tb_aes, tb_aes_core, tb_aes_decipher_block, tb_aes_encipher_block, tb_aes_key_mem. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00024&amp;file=private%2Fscoring.json'>Open private scoring contract</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Case-stored agent instruction</h2><div class='card'><p class='muted'>These are case-stored instructions. A launcher may add framing or execution limits; its saved trajectory is the exact runtime record.</p><details><summary>public/instruction.md · <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00024&amp;file=public%2Finstruction.md'>Open raw</a></summary><pre># Implement the Secworks AES project\nRead specification/project.md (the unchanged upstream README) and the declaration-only interfaces. Implement the complete seven-file AES project at the pinned revision, including encryption and decryption with 128/256-bit keys. Alternative branches described in the README are not this case.\n\nCurator interface clarification, reviewed against the pinned release before evaluation:\n- Rising-edge clock; asynchronous active-low reset_n. The 32-bit bus is word-addressed by address[7:0], not byte-addressed. Writes occur on a rising edge with cs &amp;&amp; we. Reads are combinational with cs &amp;&amp; !we; read_data is zero otherwise or for an unmapped read. No bus acknowledge exists.\n- Read 0x00/01/02 returns 0x61657320/0x20202020/0x302e3630. Write 0x08 bits 0/1 creates one-cycle registered init/next strobes. Read 0x08 returns {28&#x27;b0,keylen,encdec,next,init}. Read status 0x09 bits 0/1 is ready/result_valid. Write configuration 0x0a bits 0/1 is encdec/keylen: encdec=1 encrypts, 0 decrypts; keylen=0 selects 128, 1 selects 256. Configuration itself is read back through control bits 2/3, not 0x0a.\n- Key registers are write-only 0x10..17; block registers write-only 0x20..23. Lowest address holds the most-significant word. AES-128 uses key[255:128], the first four key words; AES-256 uses all eight. Read result at 0x30..33, most-significant word first. Reset clears wrapper data/configuration/control/result/status registers. Wrapper status/result registers follow core outputs on each edge.\n- Follow the README key-load/configure/init/wait-ready/block-load/next/wait-ready/result-read sequence. Keep key length and direction stable during an operation. Do not overlap init and next or start while busy. The API provides raw AES block operations, with no mode chaining or padding.\n- aes_core integrates aes_key_mem, aes_encipher_block, aes_decipher_block and a shared encryption/key-schedule aes_sbox. aes_sbox and aes_inv_sbox apply the standard AES byte substitution independently to the four bytes of their combinational 32-bit word ports.\n- aes_key_mem expands the standard AES key schedule into 128-bit round keys, indexed by round (0..10 for AES-128, 0..14 for AES-256); its sboxw/new_sboxw pair accesses the external combinational S-box. init starts expansion; ready reports completion. Round-key lookup is combinational. The two block engines request their round keys through round, consume round_key, and transform block into new_block with the standard forward/inverse round ordering. The encryption engine also uses its external combinational S-box ports. next starts work and ready returns high on completion. Result data is used only after completion.\n- Core reset sets ready=1 and result_valid=0. Accepting init/next clears ready and result_valid; init completion restores ready, block completion restores ready and sets result_valid until the next accepted operation. Use the documented ready handshake; private vectors include reasonable finite completion waits, not an implementation-specific internal state encoding.\n\nOriginal-document scope: README specifies algorithm, supported keys, architecture and use sequence. The address map, identity values, reset and port-level contracts above are curator clarifications from the pinned RTL, not claimed to be verbatim original prose. Required internals must implement their declared functional contracts; their state names are unrestricted.\n\nImplement every required path in output_contract.json. Declaration-only ports\nare in interfaces/modules.v. No initial/final, delays, simulation system tasks,\nforce/release or external includes are permitted. Only declared RTL is collected.\nMissing files, forbidden constructs or failed top compilation score zero. Each\nprivate point has equal weight and requires all of its checks to pass. Use\nterminal/file_editor, iverilog, vvp and Python iteratively; finish by saving RTL.\nRun `python3 checks/check.py` for public compilation feedback.\n</pre></details></div>"}, "files": [{"name": "case.json", "url": "assets/ddbbb